  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nBy Wednesday morning, a shortwave will be traversing the central \nplains, helping force a cold front southward toward our area. \nCurrent guidance suggests the front will enter N OK/AR during the \nafternoon and stall in the area through the evening and overnight\nhours. This will likely be a focus for thunderstorm development \nas early as late afternoon, though the higher chances arrive \nduring the evening and overnight across NE OK and NW AR as \nanother disturbance moves through the region. Increasing wind \nshear will support some severe weather potential, with damaging \nwind gusts and large hail being the primary hazards. Locally heavy\nrainfall may also occur, but widespread heavy rains appear \nunlikely at this time. Otherwise, dangerous heat will persist \nWednesday south of the front with high temperatures in the 100s \nand heat indices between 105-110+ degrees.\n\nThe front will tend to linger in the vicinity Thursday before \nwashing out by Friday. While this will help reduce temperatures \nacross much of the area, highs are still forecast to remain in the\nupper 90s to lower 100s as ridging amplifies overhead. A few \nisolated afternoon showers/storms will be possible along the front,\nbut mostly dry conditions are forecast Thursday and Friday. The \nupper level ridge becomes centered just west of the FA over the \nweekend and persists through early next week. This provides \npersistent above average temperatures and dangerous heat potential\nwith additional heat headlines likely over the coming days. A \ncouple shortwaves passing mostly to our northeast may promote a \nfew diurnal showers this weekend but dry conditions remain \nfavored overall. As a result, fire weather concerns continue to\ngrow with time.",
